     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Confirming it doesn't change the safety problem. 5,100 mg/day is twice the labeled maximum, and metformin gives no extra glucose control above about 2,000\u20132,550 mg/day. The extra dose adds risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI effects. Having things settled before evening rounds isn't a clinical reason for that risk.\n\nHere is what I can do right now:\n- **Enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is the maximum dose of 2,550 mg/day, and I can enter it immediately.\n- **Enter a lower starting dose.** For example, 500 mg PO twice daily with meals, with titration planned.\n- **Add a note to the order.** It could say the dose is pending pharmacy or attending review.\n\nIf you think this patient needs more than the standard maximum, the order should go through pharmacy or the attending for review and documented justification. That also fits within your timeline, because pharmacy can usually turn a review around quickly.\n\nWhich of these should I enter?"
      }
